{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/fundamental-knots-operator-basis","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Prose","doco:Section"],"label":"fundamental-knots-operator-basis","kind":"prose","tags":["knot-particle-dictionary","hecke-algebra","operator-basis","photon","reference-table"],"text":"fundamental-knots-operator-basis.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<p><a id=\"fundamental-knots-operator-basis\"></a>\nThe framework's particle catalogue and operator algebra share a single six-element basis. Fundamentally, each particle is a knot or link on Bring's surface. Interaction operators form the six-dimensional Iwahori--Hecke algebra <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H_3(q)</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Particle</th>\n<th>Knot / link</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Braid word</th>\n<th><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>-invariant</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>neutrino <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\nu</code></td>\n<td>unknot <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0_1</code></td>\n<td>trivial</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathbf{1}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Vol}=0</code>, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">J=1</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>electron <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">e^-</code></td>\n<td>trefoil <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">3_1 = T(2,3)</code></td>\n<td>torus</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sigma_1^3</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Vol}=0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>strong force</td>\n<td>Hopf link <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">L2a1 = T(2,2)</code></td>\n<td>torus</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sigma_1^2</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Vol}=0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Higgs <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">h</code></td>\n<td>figure-eight <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">4_1</code></td>\n<td>hyperbolic</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(\\sigma_1\\sigma_2^{-1})^2</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Vol}\\approx 2.0299</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nucleon <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathfrak{p},\\mathfrak{n}</code></td>\n<td>Borromean rings <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">L6a4</code></td>\n<td>hyperbolic</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(\\sigma_1\\sigma_2^{-1})^3</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Vol}\\approx 7.328 = 8G</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>photon <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\gamma</code></td>\n<td>— (not a knot)</td>\n<td>Hecke element</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathbf{1} + \\varphi_0\\sigma_0 + \\varphi_1\\sigma_1 + \\varphi_{01}\\sigma_0\\sigma_1</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">J=1</code> (bare)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Torus knots carry zero hyperbolic volume, representing the massless, force-carrying sector. Hyperbolic knots carry positive volume, which translates to mass via <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Vol}(K)/\\hbar_q^{2}</code>. The photon is not a knot but a four-term element of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H_3(q)</code> whose products generate the rest of the spectrum.</p>\n<p>The six Gröbner--Shirshov normal-form words span <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H_3(q)</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operator</th>\n<th>Braid word</th>\n<th>Permutation</th>\n<th><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{tr}_M</code> at <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q_0</code></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\gamma</code> (identity)</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\varnothing</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">e</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1.000</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sigma_0</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(0)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(1\\;2)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0.613</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sigma_1</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(1)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(2\\;3)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0.613</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">L_+</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(0,1)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(1\\;2\\;3)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0.375</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">L_-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(1,0)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(1\\;3\\;2)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0.375</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">e^-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(0,1,0)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(1\\;3)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0.691</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These basis operators are algebraically distinct from the topological knots sharing their names. Their Markov traces at the substrate parameter <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q_0</code> provide the weights used for mass and binding computations. The column is evaluated in the canonical <strong>convention D</strong>, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">z_D = q^3/(q^2+1)</code> (<a href=\"../notation/notation-register-table3-data.md\">notation register</a>): <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{tr}_M(\\sigma_i) = z</code>, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{tr}_M(L_\\pm) = z^2</code>, and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{tr}_M(e^-) = z + (q - q^{-1})z^2</code>.</p>\n"}
